# Young adults (part e): facilitator scripts, tasks and witness stories.
R = {
'YA-11-1': dict(
  saint="Blessed Pius IX: the pope of the Immaculate Conception and the First Vatican Council, whose long reign is still argued over.",
  tryit=["Test a doctrine against Newman’s notes.", "Distinguish development from change.", "Write a reply to “the Church changed its mind.”"],
  saintStory="Giovanni Maria Mastai-Ferretti was born in 1792 in Senigallia, Italy, and became pope in 1846 as Pius IX. He began as a hopeful reformer, who freed political prisoners and allowed a free press in the Papal States. But after the revolutions of 1848 and the assassination of his minister, he fled Rome. He returned under French protection and turned conservative, resisting liberalism and nationalism.\nHis reign, the longest in history at over 31 years, saw dramatic events. In 1854, he defined the dogma of the Immaculate Conception, after consulting the bishops of the world. In 1864 he issued the Syllabus of Errors, condemning a list of modern ideas, in a way that is still debated. In 1869-70 he convened the First Vatican Council, which defined papal infallibility and primacy of jurisdiction. In September 1870, the army of the new Kingdom of Italy took Rome, and he declared himself a “prisoner in the Vatican.”\nHe also made mistakes: the 1858 Mortara case, in which a Jewish boy secretly baptized by a servant was taken from his family by papal police, damaged Catholic-Jewish relations. He died in 1878 and was beatified in 2000 alongside John XXIII. His case shows the tension in dealing with a changing world.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “Has the Church ever changed her mind? Can you think of an example? Is that a sign of weakness or of honesty?” Write answers. Say, “Tonight we see how the Church understands infallibility, and how doctrine can develop without being corrupted.”",
    teach=["Say: “Infallibility: the Church teaches that Christ protects her from error when she teaches definitively on faith and morals. It is exercised in three ways: the Pope speaking ex cathedra; the bishops together with the Pope in a council; and the universal ordinary Magisterium. Vatican I (1870), under Pius IX, defined: when the Roman Pontiff ‘speaks ex cathedra, that is, when in the exercise of his office as shepherd and teacher of all Christians, in virtue of his supreme apostolic authority, he defines a doctrine concerning faith or morals to be held by the whole Church,’ he has the infallibility which Christ gave the Church. The conditions: as pastor of all, on faith or morals, definitively, to be held by all. Ex cathedra definitions are very rare: the Immaculate Conception (1854) and the Assumption (1950). It doesn’t mean the Pope is sinless, inspired, or right about everything.”", "Say: “Development of doctrine. Newman’s Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine (1845): ‘To live is to change, and to be perfect is to have changed often.’ The Church’s teaching doesn’t change in substance but is understood more deeply, as an acorn becomes an oak. Vincent of Lérins: progress, not alteration. Newman’s seven notes of authentic development: preservation of type, continuity of principles, power of assimilation, logical sequence, anticipation of its future, conservative action on its past, chronic vigor. A corruption contradicts the earlier teaching; a development draws out what was implicit. Examples: the Trinity (from Scripture to Nicaea), Christ’s two natures (Chalcedon), the canon, Mary’s Immaculate Conception and Assumption, religious freedom (Vatican II), slavery (always against dignity but slow to be condemned clearly), the death penalty (CCC 2267, 2018).”", "Say: “Hard cases. ‘Outside the Church no salvation’: developed through the idea of baptism of desire and invincible ignorance to Vatican II’s statement. Usury: the medieval ban on interest was revised with the understanding of money and risk. Religious freedom: Pius IX’s Syllabus condemned the idea that everyone has a right to religious freedom in some senses; Dignitatis Humanae (1965) taught a civil right to religious freedom based on dignity. Critics say this is a reversal; the Church says it is a development: the earlier teaching was about error having no rights, the later about persons having rights. How do we respond? Humility: not everything is yet understood; honesty: the Church has made prudential errors and sometimes expressed truths in inadequate ways; distinguish dogmas from disciplines and prudential judgments; read documents in context. The test: is it consistent with Scripture and Tradition? Not every change is a development, and not every continuity is rigidity.”"],
    ask=["What are the three ways infallibility is exercised? (The Pope ex cathedra; the bishops with the Pope in council; the universal ordinary Magisterium.)", "What is development of doctrine? (Deeper understanding of the same truth, not a change in substance.)", "What is the difference between development and corruption? (Development draws out what was implicit; corruption contradicts.)"],
    practice=["Choose one doctrine (the Immaculate Conception, the Trinity, religious freedom). Trace it from Scripture to today in a timeline.", "Test it against two of Newman’s notes.", "Write a short reply to someone who says, ‘The Church changed its teaching.’", "Share and critique with a partner."],
    close="Pray the Creed. Sign of the Cross. Suggest the group read a section of Newman’s Essay on Development or a summary.")),

'YA-11-2': dict(
  saint="Blessed Bernhard Lichtenberg: a Berlin priest who prayed publicly for the Jews after Kristallnacht.",
  tryit=["Make a list of the Church’s historical failures and good deeds.", "Describe how the Church has repented.", "Write a response to a friend who raises one."],
  saintStory="Bernhard Lichtenberg was born in 1875 in Silesia. He became a priest and in 1932 provost of St. Hedwig’s Cathedral in Berlin. A critic of Nazism from early on, he preached against the regime’s anti-Christian ideology and defended the rights of the persecuted. \nAfter the night of November 9-10, 1938 (Kristallnacht), when synagogues were burned across Germany and Jewish shops were smashed, Lichtenberg decided to act. That evening, at the end of the regular evening prayers, he told the congregation that from then on they would pray publicly “for the Jews and for the poor prisoners in the concentration camps.” He did so every evening for three years.\nHe protested to the authorities about the killing of the mentally ill, and the persecution of Jews. In October 1941, he was denounced by two young women who had heard him pray. He was arrested and sentenced to two years in prison. He asked to be sent to the ghetto at Łódź to minister to the Jews there. After he completed the sentence, the Gestapo rearrested him. Weak with heart disease, he died on November 5, 1943, while being transported to Dachau, in the city of Hof. He was beatified in 1996.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “We’re going to look honestly at some of the worst chapters of Church history. Some members of the group may feel defensive, and others angry. Both are welcome. The aim is truth.” Ask: “What have you heard about the Church and slavery, colonialism or anti-Jewish prejudice? How do you feel about it?” Allow honest answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is holy because of Christ, but her members are sinners. John Paul II, in the Jubilee of 2000, led a liturgy of repentance, asking forgiveness for sins of Christians in the past: ‘We forgive and we ask forgiveness.’ Naming the sins is part of faithfulness. Anti-Judaism: for centuries, many Christians blamed Jews collectively for the death of Jesus, the ‘deicide’ charge. This fed discrimination, expulsions, forced conversions, pogroms, ghettos, the Inquisition’s targeting of conversos, and set the stage for modern antisemitism, which contributed to the Holocaust, though Nazi ideology was racial and anti-Christian. Nostra Aetate (1965) repudiated antisemitism and the collective blame. The Catechism: Jesus’ death cannot be blamed on Jews as a people (CCC 597). Popes have visited synagogues; John Paul II prayed at the Western Wall in 2000.”", "Say: “Slavery. The Church condemned slavery in principle over time: Gregory of Nyssa spoke against it in the 4th century; Pope Paul III’s Sublimis Deus (1537) said the indigenous peoples of the Americas should not be enslaved; Urban VIII (1639) and Benedict XIV; Gregory XVI condemned the slave trade (1839); Leo XIII (1888) called for abolition. But Catholics also owned slaves, Church institutions benefited from slavery, and some theologians defended it, and the Church did not condemn slavery as intrinsically evil clearly until the late 19th and 20th century (Vatican II lists slavery among infamies, GS 27). Saints like Peter Claver and Bartolomé de las Casas fought it. Colonialism: missionaries brought the Gospel, built schools and hospitals, and defended natives (Las Casas, the Jesuit reductions). The Church also benefited from empire, and some missionaries accepted colonial attitudes, suppressing cultures. Residential schools in Canada and elsewhere: abuse and cultural destruction; Pope Francis apologized in 2022. Crusades, Inquisition: see Grade 8.”", "Say: “How to respond. First, honesty: don’t deny or minimize. Second, context: history is complex; avoid caricature, but context doesn’t excuse. Third, distinguish sin from teaching: the Church’s members sinned, and sometimes theologians erred; but her doctrine in its core (the dignity of every person) is what condemned these evils. Fourth, remember the heroes: Lichtenberg, Claver, Las Casas, Bakhita, Righteous Among the Nations who were Catholics. Fifth, repentance and reform: the Church has apologized, changed, and sought reconciliation, and work continues with indigenous communities, Jewish partners and Black Catholic communities. Sixth, solidarity: how can we address injustice now? Practical: learn the history; listen to descendants; support reconciliation efforts. Read ‘Memory and Reconciliation’ (International Theological Commission, 2000). Don’t use history as a weapon or a shield. And pray. Lichtenberg shows what faithful Catholics did.”"],
    ask=["What did John Paul II do in the Jubilee of 2000? (Led the Church in asking forgiveness for past sins of Christians.)", "What does the Catechism say about blame for Jesus’ death? (It can’t be placed on the Jewish people as a whole.)", "How should we respond to hard history? (Honestly, with context, repentance and action.)"],
    practice=["Divide a sheet into three columns: failures, repentance, heroes. Fill in at least three items each for anti-Judaism, slavery and colonialism.", "In pairs, role-play a conversation with a friend who says, ‘The Church is hypocritical because of her history.’", "Look up the work of one organization for reconciliation (e.g., Catholic-Jewish dialogue, indigenous healing).", "Write a prayer of repentance and hope."],
    close="Pray Lichtenberg’s prayer for the persecuted: “Lord, have mercy on those who suffer, and on us all.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to read the Jubilee liturgy of 2000.")),

'YA-11-3': dict(
  saint="Saints Francisco and Jacinta Marto: two Portuguese shepherd children who prayed the Rosary for sinners.",
  tryit=["Lay out the Marian dogmas with their roots.", "Say what each says about Christ.", "Write why Catholics honor Mary."],
  saintStory="In 1917, in the village of Fátima, Portugal, three shepherd children—Lúcia dos Santos (10), and her cousins Francisco (9) and Jacinta Marto (7)—reported seeing a lady “brighter than the sun” on the thirteenth of each month from May to October. She asked them to pray the Rosary every day for peace and the conversion of sinners, and to offer sacrifices. On October 13, a crowd of some 70,000 people gathered, and many reported seeing the sun appear to spin and dance in the sky.\nThe children took her request seriously. They gave their lunch to the poor, endured thirst, wore a rough rope around their waists, and spent hours in prayer. Francisco liked to “console Jesus” by praying alone before the tabernacle. Jacinta, deeply moved by a vision of sinners suffering, offered many sacrifices.\nBoth fell ill in the 1918 influenza epidemic. Francisco died on April 4, 1919, at ten. Jacinta died on February 20, 1920, at nine, after painful treatment, alone in a hospital in Lisbon. They were canonized by Pope Francis in 2017, the youngest non-martyr saints in the Church. The Church approved the Fátima apparitions as worthy of belief, but no Catholic is required to believe them.",
  script=dict(
    open="Place an image of Mary on the table, any artwork or icon, and invite each person to say one word that comes to mind. Write the words on the board. Say, “These are our starting points.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church has four Marian dogmas. Mother of God (Theotokos): Mary is truly the mother of Jesus, who is God (Council of Ephesus, 431). Luke 1:43: Elizabeth calls her ‘the mother of my Lord.’ Perpetual virginity: Mary was a virgin before, during and after the birth of Jesus (Lateran Council, 649). Isaiah 7:14, Luke 1:34. Immaculate Conception: Mary was preserved from original sin from the first moment of her conception, in view of Christ’s merits (Pius IX, 1854). Luke 1:28: ‘full of grace’ (kecharitomene) means she was already graced. Assumption: at the end of her life, Mary was taken body and soul into heavenly glory (Pius XII, 1950). Revelation 12; ancient feast, from the 5th-6th centuries. Each dogma is about Christ and about us: Mary is the first and perfect disciple, what the Church will be.”", "Say: “Why honor Mary? Because Jesus did: he obeyed her, and from the cross gave her to John: ‘Behold, your mother!’ (John 19:27) Because God chose her: ‘all generations will call me blessed.’ (Luke 1:48) Because she leads to Jesus: ‘Do whatever he tells you.’ (John 2:5) Because she is our model of faith and prayer. The Church distinguishes veneration (hyperdulia, special honor) from adoration (latria, for God alone). Catholics do not worship Mary. We ask her to pray for us, as we ask fellow Christians. Practices: the Rosary, Angelus, Hail Mary, Memorare, Marian feasts, scapular. The Rosary: twenty mysteries, from the Gospel. Marian apparitions (Guadalupe, Lourdes, Fátima): private revelations approved by the Church as worthy of belief, but they don’t add to the faith.”", "Say: “Common objections. ‘Brothers and sisters of Jesus’ in the Gospels: the words (adelphoi) can mean relatives; Hebrew ach is broader; the Gospels never call them Mary’s sons. ‘Mary needed a Savior’ (Luke 1:47): yes, and was saved by anticipation, preserved from sin. ‘Not in Scripture’: the Assumption is in Tradition; the Church sees hints in Revelation 12 and Psalm 132:8; the Immaculate Conception in ‘full of grace.’ ‘Isn’t this too much?’ The Church warns against both excess and neglect; Vatican II’s Lumen Gentium chapter 8 is balanced. Mary’s place is in the mystery of Christ and the Church. Fátima children: they show that devotion to Mary is about prayer, sacrifice and love for sinners. Mary is also the mother of the Church. If you find devotion hard, start with the Hail Mary slowly, or the Magnificat. Ask her: ‘Show me your Son.’”"],
    ask=["Name the four Marian dogmas. (Mother of God, perpetual virginity, Immaculate Conception, Assumption.)", "What is the difference between veneration and adoration? (Veneration is honor; adoration is for God alone.)", "Are Marian apparitions required to be believed? (No; they are private revelations.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart of the four dogmas: statement, Scripture, date, what it says about Christ.", "Pray one decade of the Rosary, with a Gospel scene, in silence and aloud.", "Write a short reply to a friend who says Catholics worship Mary.", "Choose one Marian prayer to pray daily this month."],
    close="Pray the Memorare together. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to read the Magnificat (Luke 1:46-55) each day for a week.")),

'YA-12-1': dict(
  saint="Padre Pio: a Capuchin who obeyed when the Church forbade him to say Mass in public.",
  tryit=["Work through a conscience-versus-teaching case.", "Identify the questions to ask first.", "Write what faithful disagreement looks like."],
  saintStory="Padre Pio of Pietrelcina became a household name in the 1920s, when thousands flocked to the friary at San Giovanni Rotondo to see the priest with the stigmata and to confess to him. Some Church officials were suspicious. In 1923, the Holy Office, which investigated these matters, imposed restrictions on him; in 1931, he was forbidden to celebrate Mass publicly or hear confessions, and was almost confined to his room. He was suspected of fraud and self-inflicted wounds, among other things.\nPadre Pio believed the accusations were false and hurtful. He could have resisted or caused a stir. He had a huge following of supporters who would have defended him. Instead, he said, “I will obey.” He said Mass privately in the friary chapel, accepted the restrictions without public complaint, and told his followers to be calm. After two years, in 1933, Pope Pius XI quietly restored his faculties.\nLater, in the 1960s, new restrictions were imposed again, and he accepted them again. His obedience is one reason the Church sees his holiness as authentic. He died in 1968 and was canonized in 2002.",
  script=dict(
    open="Present a short case: a Catholic doctor is asked to do a procedure she believes is wrong. Ask, “What are her options? What should she consider first?” Take ideas before opening the lesson.",
    teach=["Say: “Conscience. The Catechism: ‘Moral conscience is a judgment of reason by which the human person recognizes the moral quality of a concrete act.’ (CCC 1778) We must always follow a certain conscience. ‘Man has the right to act in conscience and in freedom.’ But conscience must be formed: it’s not just a feeling or preference. Newman: ‘Conscience has rights because it has duties.’ He also said that if he were to toast religion, he would toast conscience first and the Pope afterwards, meaning conscience rightly understood is the voice of God. A well-formed conscience will listen to Scripture, the Church’s teaching, and wise counsel. It’s a mistake to see conscience and Church teaching as opposed. The Church’s moral teaching is meant to inform conscience, not replace it.”", "Say: “Obedience. In Catholic life, obedience is not blind. It’s a virtue rooted in trust that the Church has Christ’s authority (Luke 10:16). The levels of assent: for dogmas, the assent of faith; for definitive teachings, firm acceptance; for non-definitive authoritative teaching, ‘religious submission of will and intellect.’ (LG 25) That means a presumption in favor of the teaching and a willingness to study. Disagreement with prudential judgments or disciplines, such as how to organize a diocese or what the Pope says about economic policy, is legitimate. Disagreement with defined teaching is a problem. What do you do if you struggle? (1) Study: do I understand the teaching and its reasons? (2) Pray. (3) Seek counsel: a priest, a teacher. (4) Distinguish the doctrine from how it was presented or lived. (5) Remain in communion; keep the sacraments. (6) Avoid public dissent that confuses others. (7) Be patient: many saints and theologians had difficulties for years.”", "Say: “Examples of faithful disagreement. Catherine of Siena urged the pope to return to Rome. Thomas More refused to sign an oath. Teresa of Avila and John of the Cross endured suspicion. Yves Congar and Henri de Lubac were silenced in the 1950s but later helped shape Vatican II. Padre Pio. Mary Ward. These people criticized or were criticized, but they remained loyal and loved the Church. The test: do I act with humility? Do I listen? Do I love the Church? Do I seek truth? When conscience and the law conflict, as for Franz Jägerstätter or the martyrs, follow conscience, accepting the cost. For most of us, the issue is smaller: a hard teaching on sexuality or authority. Don’t leave when it’s hard; ask, wrestle, wait. The Church is a family, and families argue. Obedience, rightly understood, is listening (ob-audire). Padre Pio’s humility won more than argument could.”"],
    ask=["Must we always follow our conscience? (Yes, but it must be well formed.)", "What does ‘religious submission of mind and will’ mean? (A presumption in favor of authoritative teaching, with willingness to study.)", "How did Padre Pio respond to unjust restrictions? (He obeyed without public protest.)"],
    practice=["Work through a case: a Catholic believes the Church is wrong about a teaching. List steps they should take.", "Write your own questions about a teaching you find difficult, and who you can ask.", "Write a statement of what faithful disagreement looks like and doesn’t.", "Commit to finding one answer this month."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give me a humble heart and a well-formed conscience.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to speak with a priest about a hard question.")),

'YA-12-2': dict(
  saint="Venerable Robert Schuman: a French foreign minister who rebuilt Europe with reconciliation and daily Mass.",
  tryit=["Apply Catholic principles to a policy issue.", "Name two goods that compete.", "Write what a faithful citizen does."],
  saintStory="Robert Schuman was born in 1886 in Luxembourg, to a family from Lorraine, the region disputed between France and Germany. He studied law and became a politician. His region was German when he was born, French after World War I. He served in the French Parliament, was imprisoned by the Gestapo in World War II, escaped, and hid in monasteries.\nAfter the war, as France’s foreign minister, he faced the question of how to prevent another war between France and Germany. On May 9, 1950, he announced a plan to place the coal and steel industries of both countries under a common authority, so that war between them would become “not merely unthinkable, but materially impossible.” This became the European Coal and Steel Community in 1951, the seed of the European Union.\nSchuman never married. He went to Mass daily and was known for personal modesty. He said that democracy owes its existence to Christianity, and that politics is a service. He died in 1963. In 2021, Pope Francis declared him Venerable.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “Can a person of faith be involved in politics? Should they? What are the risks of faith in politics? The risks of leaving it out?” Take answers. Say, “Tonight we find a Catholic way of thinking.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that political participation is a duty: ‘It is necessary that all participate, each according to his position and role, in promoting the common good.’ (CCC 1913-1915) Faithful Citizenship, the US bishops’ document, says conscience should guide Catholic voters. Vatican II: Christians shouldn’t flee their duties in the world (GS 43). Politics is a ‘demanding form of charity’ (Paul VI). The Church doesn’t endorse parties or candidates, but she forms consciences with principles: the dignity of every person from conception to natural death; the common good; subsidiarity; solidarity; the option for the poor; the family as the basic unit; religious freedom; care for creation. These principles don’t map neatly onto party platforms. A Catholic may find herself pulled in different directions.”", "Say: “The hard part: weighing priorities. The bishops teach that some issues involve ‘intrinsically evil’ acts that can never be supported, like abortion and euthanasia, and these have a special seriousness; other issues, like how to reduce poverty or care for immigrants, involve prudential judgment about the best means, though the goal is fixed. Catholics can disagree on the means. Pope Francis and the bishops warn against reducing Catholic teaching to a single issue, and against ignoring any. A voter must form conscience, study the facts, pray, and weigh. Voting for a candidate because of a position one disagrees with, for proportionate reasons, can be acceptable, but never to support an intrinsic evil as such. Practical: read primary sources, not just partisan media; talk with people who disagree; avoid contempt; pay attention to local issues; vote; run for office; join a civic group.”", "Say: “The Church and the state. The Church’s traditional teaching: Church and state are distinct; each has its own sphere (Jesus: ‘Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar’s, and to God the things that are God’s,’ Mark 12:17). Religious freedom. Catholic social teaching also warns against both the secular state that excludes religion and the confessional state that coerces faith. Lay Catholics have the primary role in politics; clergy should form consciences, not lead parties. Be careful of idolatry of party or ideology, tribal politics and hatred. Remember Christians in all parties. Pray for leaders (1 Timothy 2:1-2). Schuman’s approach: reconciliation, patience, humility, service. Practical: choose one issue to learn about; contact your representatives; vote with a formed conscience; speak charitably online. Faith is not private, but it is not partisan.”"],
    ask=["Is political participation a Catholic duty? (Yes, according to one’s role.)", "Does the Church endorse parties? (No; she forms consciences with principles.)", "What is the difference between an intrinsic evil and a prudential judgment? (Intrinsic evils are always wrong; prudential issues involve judging the best means.)"],
    practice=["Choose a current policy issue. Apply Catholic principles (dignity, common good, subsidiarity, solidarity) and list how they pull.", "Write what facts you need to learn before deciding.", "Write a respectful way to discuss it with someone who disagrees.", "Choose one civic action for this semester: register, volunteer, contact an official."],
    close="Pray for leaders: “Lord, guide those who govern, and make us faithful citizens.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to read Faithful Citizenship.")),

'YA-12-3': dict(
  saint="Mother Teresa of Calcutta: she served for decades in the dark, without the felt presence of God, and said she had come to love the darkness.",
  tryit=["Describe your own dry season in prayer.", "List what has helped in the past.", "Write a plan for the next one."],
  saintStory="In 1946, on a train to Darjeeling, Mother Teresa received what she called a call within her vocation: to serve Jesus in the poorest of the poor. She founded the Missionaries of Charity in Calcutta in 1950. In the early years, she experienced visions and consolation. But beginning in 1948-49 and lasting, with brief breaks, until her death in 1997, she experienced a deep spiritual darkness: a sense of God’s absence, emptiness and abandonment.\nShe did not reveal this publicly. After her death, her letters were published in Mother Teresa: Come Be My Light (2007). In a letter to a priest friend, she wrote: “Jesus has a very special love for you. As for me, the silence and the emptiness is so great that I look and do not see, listen and do not hear.” She wrote that she was in “terrible darkness,” and doubted God. Some people took this as a sign she lost faith. But she continued to pray, serve and go to Mass daily. She came to see the darkness as a share in the thirst of Jesus on the cross, and wrote that she had “come to love the darkness,” because she believed it was a part of his suffering.\nShe was canonized in 2016. Her story is a witness to fidelity without feeling.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “Be honest about your inner life. On a scale of 1 to 10, how close does God feel right now? Write a number on a slip of paper. You don’t need to share it.” Allow silence. Then: “If it’s low, you are in good company.”",
    teach=["Say: “Doubt and dryness are common. The Catechism distinguishes: involuntary doubt (difficulty in believing, which is not a sin) and voluntary doubt (deliberately fostering uncertainty). The father in Mark 9:24 says, ‘I believe; help my unbelief!’ Thomas doubted and was answered. Dryness in prayer is when we feel nothing, or find prayer boring. Reasons include fatigue, stress, sin, or God’s way of purifying our love. The ‘dark night of the soul,’ described by John of the Cross, is a stage when God withdraws consolations to draw us to a purer faith, hope and love. It differs from depression, though they can overlap; if sadness, hopelessness or loss of interest in everything persist, seek medical or psychological help, as well as spiritual guidance. Don’t assume it’s only a spiritual problem.”", "Say: “What to do. One: persevere. The measure of prayer is faithfulness, not feeling. Pray even briefly; keep your time. Two: simplify. Use short prayers: ‘Jesus, I trust in you.’ The Psalms give words; the lament psalms speak to feelings of abandonment (Psalm 22, 88). Three: keep the sacraments. The Eucharist and Confession don’t depend on feelings. Four: keep serving: love is shown in deeds. Five: seek a spiritual director or a wise friend; don’t isolate. Six: examine your life: is there unconfessed sin, neglected duty, unforgiveness, overwork? Seven: take care of the body: sleep, exercise, nutrition. Eight: read the saints who went through it: Teresa of Calcutta, Thérèse of Lisieux, Jane de Chantal, John of the Cross. Nine: trust. Even Jesus on the cross prayed, ‘My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?’ Faith is not a feeling; it’s a decision to trust.”", "Say: “Doubts about the faith itself. Honest intellectual doubts deserve honest engagement: read serious authors, talk to someone knowledgeable, and keep praying while you search. Doubts often come with life changes (college, loss, hurt). Doubts due to scandal or suffering: speak to a pastor. Doubts about whether God loves you: talk to someone. Don’t make major decisions in desolation, Ignatius advised; wait for consolation. Remember what you knew in light. Encourage others in their dryness: don’t say ‘just pray more’ but ‘I’m with you.’ Mother Teresa’s experience shows that holiness is not a constant feeling of closeness, and that God can be working deeply when we feel nothing. The course ends here; the road continues. ‘My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.’ (2 Corinthians 12:9)”"],
    ask=["Is involuntary doubt a sin? (No.)", "What is the dark night of the soul? (A stage where God withdraws consolations to purify faith and love.)", "What should you do in a dry season? (Persevere in prayer and sacraments, keep serving, seek guidance, care for body and mind.)"],
    practice=["Write about a dry season you have had: what it felt like, what helped.", "Write a short plan for the next one: one short prayer, one sacrament habit, one person to tell, one self-care practice.", "Read Psalm 88 or 22 slowly. Notice that the psalm is honest.", "Share one line of your plan with a partner."],
    close="Pray Mother Teresa’s prayer: “Jesus, I trust in you,” and end with the Our Father. Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to keep meeting, to continue the journey together.")),
}
